From c6e0236b081528298bf6bb9a3e3df8175485bf74 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kryxzort <101042137+Kryxzort@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 09:13:37 +0530
Subject: [PATCH 127/127] render: run the shader text passes' regexes only
 where they can match
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The WE-rule passes over each shader unit (ShaderUnit::compile's varying passes, WERules' intrinsic overloads, clip,
reserved words, #if/#endif lines) ran some 30 std::regex searches over the whole unit text, most of them built per
varying or per intrinsic. libstdc++'s regex executor backtracks and copies its sub-matches at every start position:
about 1 ms per search over a 20 kB particle unit (genericparticle with LightingV1). A scene with many particle
systems compiles that unit pair once per system, so 2371103399 (36 systems) spent ~1.7 s of its 2.5 s build in
regex; it built in 0.92 s before 259c1b6 (+0.6 s: the writable-varying copy, a regex per varying) and cbdf2fb
(+0.85 s: WE's preprocessing rules, the intrinsic overloads' 8 regexes and the vertex side of the copy).

Each search now first runs a hand-written check that is a necessary condition for its regex (WERules::Scan: a
whole-word occurrence with the regex's neighbours, `\bname\b` itself, a line starting `#`, …), so the regex only
runs where it can match and decides there; an iterator over declarations tries its regex only where the leading
keyword is, anchored (match_continuous + match_prev_avail: what the search finds at that position). No rule changed.

Measured (warm `liascape-scene-worker --lwe-smoke`, build_ms, alternating 8 runs each): 2371103399 2400-2559 ms
(median 2490) before, 684-803 ms (median 710) now. The WE-reference compare's 96 scenes render in 862 s instead of
990 s.

Same output: every toGlsl input (each unit's compiled text and its spliced/#endif-dropped copy) was dumped before and
after for the shader sweep (4085 checks of the stock and library shaders: 3426 distinct texts) and the 96 WE-ref
scenes (5119 compiles, 586 distinct): byte-identical, in the same order per process; the sweep's results (ok, stage,
compat records) are identical; 2371103399's frames byte-identical at 1, 1.7, 3.2 and 6.2 s. Frames that differ
between the two runs are wall-clock content (clock text a second or minute earlier: the build ended sooner under
libfaketime) or differ run to run with either core.

Origin: ours
Why: docs/TODO.md (Kimi 2371103399 builds in ~2.5 s since the WE port)
